That makes sense biomechanically. At high sprint speeds, running starts to feel less like “stepping quickly” and more like a sequence of ballistic launches.

You’re essentially doing this over and over:

\text{plant} \rightarrow \text{huge force impulse} \rightarrow \text{flight phase} \rightarrow \text{catch} \rightarrow \text{launch again}

The faster you go, the more of the stride cycle is spent airborne, and the less it feels like continuous contact with the ground. The ground becomes something you briefly hit to redirect and re-accelerate your center of mass.

A good term for the sensation is ballistic locomotion or bounding sprint mechanics. You’re not pulling yourself forward so much as repeatedly projecting yourself forward and up, then managing the landing efficiently enough to preserve speed.

That’s also why very fast sprinting can feel almost like throwing your whole body:

\text{body} \approx \text{projectile}

with each foot strike acting as the next launch event.

If you want the Pantheon version: running is continuous propulsion; sprinting at the limit becomes controlled self-throwing.